        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 976,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Takes the positions along x and the positions along y, and makes two matrices the size of the whole grid: X holds every point's x, and Y every point's y.\n\nWith those, any formula of x and y can be worked out over the whole grid at once, element by element: the distance from the centre (hypot of X and Y), a wave (sin of X), a gradient, a mask of a region - or a trained network run over every point of a plane (t.model). It is how a 2-D picture, field or terrain is computed from a formula instead of drawn point by point. The grids are made on x's device.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.meshgrid\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nx:\nThe positions along x (a list or 1-D tensor). Every input makes the grid and sends.\n\ny:\nThe positions along y, held: send them first. Without y the grid is square, using x for both.\n\nindexing:\nAn option, under the node. xy (the default) makes X and Y with one row per y and one column per x, the way an image is laid out. ij makes one row per x instead.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nX:\nEach point's x: every row the same.\n\nY:\nEach point's y: every column the same. Sent first, then X.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: x 1 2 3 4 and y 10 20 30 make a 3 x 4 grid. X repeats 1 2 3 4 down every row; Y repeats 10 20 30 across every column.\n\nBottom: 32 positions from -1 to 1 make a 32 x 32 grid, and hypot of X and Y gives each point's distance from the centre: a round glow, 0 in the middle and 1.41 in the corners.\n\nRELATED:\nt.linspace and t.arange make the positions. hypot, atan2 and the maths nodes then work on the whole grid. np.meshgrid is the numpy version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 960,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Takes the positions along x and the positions along y, and makes two matrices the size of the whole grid: X holds every point's x, and Y every point's y.\n\nWith those, any formula of x and y can be worked out over the whole grid at once, element by element: the distance from the centre (hypot of X and Y), a wave (sin of X), a gradient, a mask of a region - or a trained network run over every point of a plane (t.model). It is how a 2-D picture, field or terrain is computed from a formula instead of drawn point by point. The grids are made on x's device.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.meshgrid\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nx:\nThe positions along x (a list or 1-D tensor). Every input makes the grid and sends.\n\ny:\nThe positions along y, held: send them first. Without y the grid is square, using x for both.\n\nindexing:\nAn option, under the node. xy (the default) makes X and Y with one row per y and one column per x, the way an image is laid out. ij makes one row per x instead.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nX:\nEach point's x: every row the same.\n\nY:\nEach point's y: every column the same. Sent first, then X.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: x 1 2 3 4 and y 10 20 30 make a 3 x 4 grid. X repeats 1 2 3 4 down every row; Y repeats 10 20 30 across every column.\n\nBottom: 32 positions from -1 to 1 make a 32 x 32 grid, and hypot of X and Y gives each point's distance from the centre: a round glow, 0 in the middle and 1.41 in the corners.\n\nRELATED:\nt.linspace and t.arange make the positions. hypot, atan2 and the maths nodes then work on the whole grid. np.meshgrid is the numpy version."
        },
